 ## Background
-Convex Contouring with Decision Trees was introduced in the paper "Convex contouring of volumetric data" [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of Marching Cubes, specifically the potential for disconnected or non-convex negative space within cells, which hinders applications like collision detection [S1]. The look-up tables are constructed algorithmically based on the topology and geometry of the polyhedrons, minimizing the errors associated with manually created tables [S1]. The method has been extended to support multi-resolution contouring on nested grids [S1].
+Convex Contouring was introduced in a paper titled "Convex contouring of volumetric data" [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of Marching Cubes, particularly the lack of convexity in the negative space and the susceptibility of manually created lookup tables to errors [S1]. The algorithm uses an automatically constructed look-up table that stores compact decision trees by sign configurations, allowing for dynamic triangulation of isosurface patches [S1]. The technique has been extended to multi-resolution contouring and arbitrary convex, space-filling polyhedra [S1].
 
